  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/7c5bc75101a3bf4b4074bf60c1ab68962f2ed0de559f889365c3807be2fa833c.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above. What are the $x$-coordinates of all the critical points?",
  "worked_solution": "The critical points of $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ where ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ doesn't exist. Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal or where the tangent does not exist, respectively.\n\nIn the given graph, we can see that\n\n- at any point between $x=2$ and $x=6$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0,$ while\n\n- at $x=2$ and at $x=6$ there are sharp corners, hence ${f}^{\u2032}(2)$ and ${f}^{\u2032}(6)$ don't exist.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/6e4299d762e26ff3de85ae0fd766d28368de937098dfcf232ce9a234fee826e1.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, all numbers in the closed interval $[2,6]$ are critical points for $f(x).$",
